<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1797 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn Joseph Haydn wrote in 1797 for the Austrian emperor's birthday. Its tune is now Germany's national anthem.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Lorenz Leopold Haschka</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>Joseph Haydn</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>The Habsburg monarchy (Austria, later Austria-Hungary)</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Vienna, Austria</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first performed 12 February 1797</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Lorenz Leopold Haschka, music by Joseph Haydn (1797))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>The song was written in 1797, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> was gravely threatened by Revolutionary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. According to an account written in 1847 by Anton Schmid, custodian of the Austrian National Library, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a> had come to know &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and wished that Austria had a song like it to honour its sovereign. Count Franz von Saurau, President of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>, took up the idea: as he later wrote, he had a text made by the poet Lorenz Leopold Haschka and turned to Haydn as the one composer who could write something to stand beside the British anthem. The song was also meant to rouse Austrians to volunteer for the war against France.</p><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gott-erhalte-franz-den-kaiser">Gott erhalte Franz den Kaiser</a>&quot; was first performed on the birthday of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Emperor Francis II</a>, 12 February 1797, at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgtheater">Burgtheater</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, with the emperor present for his 29th birthday. Printed copies were sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and opera houses across the Austrian lands with instructions for performance. It proved popular and came to serve, unofficially, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s first national anthem. Haydn's sketches show how he pruned the melody to reach its climax; whether he drew on folk song, or folk singers later took up his tune, is still debated.</p><p>Haydn wrote three more versions: one for orchestra, a set of variations as the slow movement of his String Quartet Op. 76 No. 3, the &quot;Emperor&quot; quartet commissioned by Count Joseph Erdödy, and a piano arrangement of that movement published in 1799. In his last, frail years he often played the song at the piano for consolation. His servant Johann Elssler recorded that he played it three times over on 26 May 1809, the last music he played; he died on 31 May.</p><p>The tune remained the anthem of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> emperors with changing words. After Francis died in 1835 new words praised his successor Ferdinand; after Ferdinand's abdication in 1848 the original words returned, since the new emperor was also named Francis; in 1854 new words were adopted for emperor and country. There were versions in Czech, Croatian, Slovene, Hungarian, Ukrainian, Polish, Italian and other languages of the empire. Other composers quoted the melody as an emblem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and its monarchy: Beethoven in a work celebrating the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, Rossini, Paganini, Donizetti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clara-schumann">Clara Schumann</a>, Smetana and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Bruckner</a>, while Bartók used it in his symphonic poem Kossuth (1903) as the sign of the Austrian enemy of the Hungarian Revolution of 1848.</p><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> was dissolved in 1918, the new Austrian Republic set the tune aside, and in 1920 the government moved to replace it. It returned in 1929 with entirely new words as the anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a> of 1938. With its older words it kept its link to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>: the first stanza of the 1854 version was sung at the funerals of Empress Zita in 1989 and of her son Otto von Habsburg in 2011. In 1841 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-von-fallersleben">Hoffmann von Fallersleben</a> wrote &quot;Das Lied der Deutschen&quot; to Haydn's melody, and its third stanza is today the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In English hymnals the tune is known as &quot;Austria&quot; or &quot;Austrian Hymn&quot;, most often with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-newton">John Newton</a>'s &quot;Glorious Things of Thee Are Spoken&quot;.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer that God preserve Emperor Francis and his rule, written as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>'s answer to &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a>&quot; in wartime. The mood is calm and dignified; Haydn's broad melody gives it the character of a hymn rather than a march.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gott erhalte Franz, den Kaiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God preserve Franz, the Emperor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Unsern guten Kaiser Franz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our good Emperor Franz!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Lange lebe Franz, der Kaiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Long live Franz, the Emperor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">In des Glückes hellstem Glanz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>in fortune's brightest splendour!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ihm erblühen Lorbeerreiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May laurel sprigs blossom for him,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wo er geht, zum Ehrenkranz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>wherever he goes, into a wreath of honour!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gott erhalte Franz, den Kaiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God preserve Franz, the Emperor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Unsern guten Kaiser Franz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our good Emperor Franz!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Laß von seiner Fahnen Spitzen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let from the tips of his banners</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Strahlen Sieg und Furchtbarkeit!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>shine victory and dreadfulness!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Laß in seinem Rathe sitzen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Let in his council sit</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Weisheit, Klugheit, Redlichkeit!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>wisdom, prudence, honesty!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und mit Seiner Hoheit Blitzen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And with His Highness's lightning</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Schalten nur Gerechtigkeit!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>let only justice hold sway!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gott erhalte Franz, den Kaiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God preserve Franz, the Emperor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Unsern guten Kaiser Franz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our good Emperor Franz!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ströme deiner Gaben Fülle</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pour the fullness of your gifts</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Über ihn, sein Haus und Reich!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>over him, his house and realm!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Brich der Bosheit Macht, enthülle</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Break the power of wickedness, unmask</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jeden Schelm- und Bubenstreich!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>every rogue's and knave's trick!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Dein Gesetz sei stets sein Wille,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May your law always be his will,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Dieser uns Gesetzen gleich.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and this be to us as laws.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gott erhalte Franz, den Kaiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God preserve Franz, the Emperor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Unsern guten Kaiser Franz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our good Emperor Franz!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Froh erleb’ er seiner Lande,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May he joyfully live to see his lands',</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Seiner Völker höchsten Flor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>his peoples' highest flowering!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Seh sie, Eins durch Bruderbande,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>See them, one through bonds of brothers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ragen allen andern vor!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>tower above all others!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und vernehm noch an dem Rande</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And may he still hear, at the edge</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Später Gruft der Enkel Chor:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>of a late grave, his grandchildren's chorus:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Gott erhalte Franz, den Kaiser,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>God preserve Franz, the Emperor,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Unsern guten Kaiser Franz!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>our good Emperor Franz!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Lorenz Leopold Haschka (1797). Text from <a href="https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/%C3%96sterreichische_Kaiserhymnen" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>line 1.1: 'erhalte' = preserve, keep (in life and power); 'Franz' is Francis II, Holy Roman Emperor (from 1804 Francis I of Austria), kept in its German form.</li><li>line 1.5: 'Lorbeerreiser' = laurel twigs, the victor's laurels; 'zum Ehrenkranz' = to make a wreath of honour.</li><li>line 2.2: 'Furchtbarkeit' = the quality of inspiring fear, terribleness.</li><li>line 2.5-6: 'Seiner Hoheit Blitzen' = the lightning (flashes) of His Majesty's power; 'schalten' = rule, hold sway: let his power strike only as justice directs.</li><li>line 3.4: 'Schelm- und Bubenstreich' = a rogue's trick and a knave's trick (villainy, treachery), not a harmless prank.</li><li>line 3.6: 'Dieser uns Gesetzen gleich' = and may this (his will) be equal to laws for us.</li><li>line 4.2: 'Flor' = bloom, flourishing, prosperity.</li><li>line 4.5-6: 'an dem Rande später Gruft' = at the edge of a late grave, i.e. in a long old age; 'Enkel' = grandchildren, also descendants in general.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">String Quartet Op. 76 No. 3, &quot;Emperor&quot; (Haydn)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1797</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations (slow movement)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Die Schlacht bei Leipzig (Philipp Jakob Riotte)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1814</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Es ist vollbracht, WoO 97 (Beethoven, finale of Treitschke's Die Ehrenpforten)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1815</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations, Op. 73 (Carl Czerny)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1824</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Il viaggio a Reims (Rossini)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1825</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Maestosa Sonata Sentimentale (Paganini)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1828</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Maria Stuarda (Donizetti)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1835</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Souvenir de Vienne, Op. 9 (Clara Schumann)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1838</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1841</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Festive Symphony (Smetana)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1853</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Variations on the Austrian National Anthem, Op. 10 (Wieniawski)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1853</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Concert variations, Op. 3 (John Knowles Paine)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1860</span><span class="sg-chain-h">variations</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arrangement for orchestra (Tchaikovsky)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1874</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arranged</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Improvisationskizze Ischl 1890 (Bruckner)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1890</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kossuth (Bartók)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1903</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphonia Germanica (Erwin Schulhoff)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1919</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sei gesegnet ohne Ende</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1929</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody, new words (anthem of Austria until 1938)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Glorious Things of Thee Are Spoken (John Newton)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (hymn tune &quot;Austria&quot;)</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Stand, Columbia</span><span class="sg-chain-h">same melody (alma mater of Columbia University)</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><a href="/songs/god-save-the-king">God Save the King</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1744</span><span class="sg-chain-h">model (Haydn and Saurau wanted an Austrian song to match it)</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec"><h2>People, places and events</h2><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-haydn">Joseph Haydn</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Lorenz Leopold Haschka</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-ii">Francis II</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Franz von Saurau</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Anton Schmid</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Johann Elssler</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Joseph Erdödy</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Charles Burney</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-austria">Ferdinand I of Austria</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Francis Joseph I</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Charles I of Austria</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Otto von Habsburg</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Zita of Bourbon-Parma</span><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">August Heinrich Hoffmann von Fallersleben</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludwig-van-beethoven">Ludwig van Beethoven</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Gioachino Rossini</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-paganini">Niccolò Paganini</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Gaetano Donizetti</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clara-schumann">Clara Schumann</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedrich-smetana">Bedřich Smetana</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyotr-ilyich-tchaikovsky">Pyotr Ilyich Tchaikovsky</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Anton Bruckner</a><span class="tih-tag sg-tag-plain">Béla Bartók</span><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-newton">John Newton</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgtheater">Burgtheater</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a><a class="tih-tag